WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.765
Secure TypeScript applications need data models that describe more than property names.

2
00:00:05.765 --> 00:00:13.654
A request model, response model, DTO, domain object, or configuration object should make important assumptions visible.

3
00:00:13.654 --> 00:00:16.688
Which fields are required? Which are optional?

4
00:00:16.688 --> 00:00:20.557
Which values are allowed? Which strings have length limits?

5
00:00:20.557 --> 00:00:24.426
Which numbers have bounds? Which dates need normalization?

6
00:00:24.426 --> 00:00:29.129
Validation should happen close to the boundary and before sensitive use.

7
00:00:29.129 --> 00:00:39.141
Request data should be checked before authorization decisions, database queries, file operations, workflow state changes, or downstream service calls.

8
00:00:39.141 --> 00:00:46.424
A client-controlled field should not become trusted simply because the frontend was also written in TypeScript.

9
00:00:46.424 --> 00:00:49.610
The server still needs to verify what it receives.

10
00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.340
There are different layers of validation.

11
00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:56.133
Shape validation checks whether the structure is present.

12
00:00:56.133 --> 00:01:05.843
Meaning validation checks whether values make sense: a status is allowed, a date is valid, an array is not too large, or a string is in the expected format.

13
00:01:05.843 --> 00:01:13.201
Permission validation asks whether the current user or system context is allowed to use that value for this action.

14
00:01:13.201 --> 00:01:16.235
Default values and normalization also matter.

15
00:01:16.235 --> 00:01:23.062
Trimming, case handling, date parsing, enum conversion, and empty-field handling should be consistent.

16
00:01:23.062 --> 00:01:30.799
Validation errors should be predictable enough for callers to respond, but they should not reveal sensitive internals.

17
00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:39.143
Good data models preserve application invariants by rejecting or normalizing data before it reaches code that assumes it is safe.

18
00:01:39.143 --> 00:01:43.619
Validation should also be connected to the domain, not only to syntax.

19
00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:47.563
A number can be a number and still be outside the allowed range.

20
00:01:47.563 --> 00:01:51.887
A string can be present and still be too long for a downstream system.

21
00:01:51.887 --> 00:01:57.349
An identifier can have the right format and still belong to a different user or tenant.

22
00:01:57.349 --> 00:02:04.327
Secure data modeling keeps those distinctions visible across handlers, services, and storage boundaries.
